# Sound effects
@scenario tutorial_lesson_06
@include /definitions/characters.vns
@label start
[mode dialogue standard]
[set ui.dialogueUi project]
[bg room]
[char ginger global on]
[set ui.dialogueFadeMs 160]
[stage clear]
[show ginger beside_code normal]
[set tutorial_source scripts/tutorial/06_audio_and_music.vns]
[set tutorial_excerpt before]
[set ui.dialogueFacet tutorial_code]
ginger: This lesson plays {b}assets/tutorial/welcome.wav{/b} twice. Watch where each sfx command sits relative to the text.
# region before
[sfx assets/tutorial/welcome.wav]
ginger: The first sound was triggered before this line appeared.
# endregion
[show ginger beside_code thoughtful]
[set tutorial_excerpt after]
# region after
ginger: The second sound will be triggered when you advance past this line.
[sfx assets/tutorial/welcome.wav]
# endregion
ginger: A dialogue line waits for input. Moving an sfx command across that line changes which click triggers the sound.
ginger: Try using another short WAV in the second sfx command. Keep the first one as a reference. If only one plays, check the new path and filename.
ginger: Trace the order in the source panel. A sound placed above a dialogue line can start as that line opens. A sound below it waits for the player to finish that line and advance.
ginger: You can use the same WAV for both tests. This isolates the effect of moving the command from changes in volume, duration or leading silence in the file.
[clear ui.dialogueFacet]
> Put sound on the correct click -> practice
> Review question -> check
> Back to topics -> return_hub
@label check
ginger: Which command plays a short sound effect?
> bgm -> retry
> sfx -> correct
> Back to topics -> return_hub
@label retry
[show ginger guide confused]
ginger: Bgm selects background music. Look at the command used for the two one-shot sounds.
[jump check]
@label correct
[show ginger guide normal]
ginger: Use sfx with the path to the audio file.
> Back to topics -> return_hub
> Replay example -> start
@label return_hub
[clear ui.dialogueFacet]
[if !tutorial_done_06]
[flag tutorial_done_06]
[inc tutorial_count]
[endif]
[stage clear]
[goto TutorialHub:topics_page_3]
[end]

@label practice
[show ginger beside_code thoughtful]
[set tutorial_source scripts/tutorial/06_audio_and_music.vns]
[set tutorial_excerpt after]
[set ui.dialogueFacet tutorial_code]
ginger: Suppose a confirmation sound should play when an instruction appears. Put the sound immediately above the instruction. Start one line earlier and advance into it.
ginger: Move the command immediately below that instruction and repeat. Leave the instruction open for a moment. The sound should now occur when you leave it.
ginger: If neither version plays, try the known welcome WAV at the same command. A working reference clip points you toward the replacement file or its path.
ginger: If both play at an unexpected time, trace the preceding line that waits for input. A preview that begins directly on the dialogue can skip a sound command above the cursor.
@label practice_question
ginger: The sound should mark dismissal of an instruction. Where does the command go?
> Immediately before the instruction. -> practice_hint
> Immediately after the instruction. -> practice_result
> Back to topics -> return_hub
@label practice_hint
ginger: A command above the text can run before the player has read it. Follow the order from the advance that dismisses the line.
[jump practice_question]
@label practice_result
ginger: The instruction holds execution until advance. The sound is the next instruction reached once that hold ends.
[clear ui.dialogueFacet]
> Review question -> check
> Try the exercise again -> practice
> Back to topics -> return_hub
